source: https://www.facebook.com/share/v/1CHC1bhDCJ/
yield: 6 naan, ~85g dough balls
ingredients:
  - item: warm water
    mass: 120
    qty: 0.5
    unit: cup
  - item: yeast
    mass: 3
    qty: 1
    unit: tsp
  - item: plain yogurt
    mass: 80
    qty: 0.33
    unit: cup
  - item: unsalted butter
    mass: 28
    qty: 2
    unit: tbsp
    note: melted
  - item: sugar
    mass: 8
    qty: 2
    unit: tsp
  - item: salt
    mass: 5
    note: amount not given in the original — ~2% of the flour
  - item: bread flour
    mass: 260
    qty: 2
    unit: cup
    basis: true
  - item: oil
    mass: 40
    qty: 3
    unit: tbsp
    note: the original lists 3 tbsp but only says to put a little on the dough — use what it takes to coat the dough and bowl
  - item: ghee
    mass: 15
    note: to cook the naans, ~1/2 tsp a side
components:
  - name: dough
    steps:
      - mix water, yeast, yogurt, butter and sugar
      - when cool (lukewarm, not hot from the butter) add salt and bread flour
      - mix everything in a dough mixer or by hand, ~5-8 mins until smooth and soft
      - put a little oil on it, cover it and keep it in a warm place for at least an hour or until it doubles
      - cut into 6 equal balls (~85g), cover and let it rise for half an hour
  - name: garnish
    ingredients:
      - item: ghee
        mass: 26
        qty: 2
        unit: tbsp
        note: melted
      - item: roasted garlic
        mass: 5
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
        note: mashed
      - item: cilantro
        mass: 2
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
        note: chopped
    steps:
      - mix ghee, garlic and cilantro
  - name: cook
    steps:
      - roll each dough ball into an oval shape with little dry flour, ~5mm thick
      - heat a flat pan on medium high heat (~200°C)
      - cook it with applying a little ghee on both sides until light brown, ~1-2 mins a side — it should bubble up on the first side before you flip
      - before serving brush it with ghee/garlic/cilantro mixture
notes: |
  From Ma's Kitchen. The original is in cups and spoons; the grams are conversions (bread
  flour at 130g/cup). The salt amount, kneading time, rolled thickness, pan temp and cook
  times aren't in the original and are filled in.
